section('13. Reconstructing the workflow',f'''To understand or continue the work, follow the dependency order:

1. Read the shared style-guide chat and compare the concept images before treating any image as a modeling blueprint.
2. Inspect the original Blender source and the six staged environment scripts. Work on a backup, not by re-running scene-clearing bootstrap code inside the finished live project.
3. Use the original asset folder for source comparison, and the project-side normalized FBX/materials for the currently implemented Unity map. Keep source versus imported geometry counts distinct.
4. Preserve the active Unity scene and inspect URP settings before rebuilding materials or post-processing. The Editor setup script is a reference, not a safe unchanged “new scene” command.
5. Reuse the existing rigged assets and validated animation controllers; keep visual appearance, animation and world locomotion as separate layers of responsibility.
6. Use an explicit Unity project path for connection and commands. Example command family used here: `unity command script run --project-path '/Users/pranay/My project' -- --file '<local-editor-script.cs>' --entry '<Class.Method>'`. Exact tool options/version should be checked before replaying historical commands.
7. Save the map before WebGL builds, validate the generated distribution and keep its data/code versions together. Updating Blender or Unity alone does not update the public website.
8. For publication, reuse the existing Sites identity and audience. Do not upload the whole working folder or private backups as the browser distribution.

This is a reconstruction guide derived from the saved work, not a claim that every command was issued verbatim in this order. The recovered shared chat includes the original image/character briefs; the inspected scripts and audits demonstrate the implementation.''')

